cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use flyverse, please cite it as below, and cite the MaleCNS v1.0 connectome release and Shiu et al. 2024 as well -- flyverse is a simulator built on their data and their neuron model. If you use the female backends, cite the FlyWire FAFB v783 and/or BANC v888 releases and their original reconstruction papers."
title: "flyverse: the MaleCNS fly connectome in simulations, games and other strange places"
abstract: "A whole-CNS Drosophila simulation driven by the Janelia FlyEM MaleCNS v1.0 connectome: a graded optic lobe feeding a leaky integrate-and-fire central brain and VNC, embodied in a ray-traced room, with an interpretability toolkit for localizing behavioural deficits to populations, synapses and links, an optional instrumented preset whose labelled stand-ins each name their gap, their law (sourced, or marked unverified) and the result that would retire them -- beside the unchanged raw default, whose bit-identity is a CPU claim -- and three connectome backends (MaleCNS v1.0, FlyWire FAFB v783, BANC v888)."
type: software
authors:
  # An entity author (a handle, not a legal name); the GitHub account is tel-0s -- deliberately NOT in
  # `alias:`, because cffconvert renders the alias in place of the name ("tel-0s (2026)").
  - name: "tel0s"
# Kept equal to pyproject.toml's `version` (tests/test_packaging.py::CitationTests
# ::test_citation_version_tracks_pyproject); `date-released` is the day 0.2.0 was cut.
version: "0.2.0"
date-released: "2026-09-19"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/tel-0s/flyverse"
url: "https://github.com/tel-0s/flyverse"
keywords:
  - connectomics
  - Drosophila
  - MaleCNS
  - spiking-neural-network
  - leaky-integrate-and-fire
  - neural-simulation
  - embodied-simulation
  - interpretability

references:
  # The dataset flyverse runs on. Cited as the data release; the files flyverse reads and their SHA-256
  # hashes are listed in flyverse/data/manifest.json (fetched by scripts/fetch_data.py).
  # TODO: confirm and add the DOI of the MaleCNS release paper before the public announcement. The
  # manifest records it as "Cell 2026, S0092-8674(26)00942-6"; that PII has not been checked against a
  # registered DOI, so no `doi:` field is claimed here. Also confirm the data licence (the download page
  # said CC BY 4.0 at the time of writing) and the canonical author list for the release.
  - type: data
    title: "MaleCNS v1.0: the adult Drosophila melanogaster male central nervous system connectome (flat connectome release)"
    authors:
      - name: "Janelia FlyEM Project Team"
    year: 2026
    version: "1.0"
    url: "https://storage.googleapis.com/flyem-male-cns/"
    repository: "https://male-cns.janelia.org/download/"
    notes: "Janelia Research Campus, HHMI. Release paper DOI to be confirmed (see the TODO above)."

  # The two female releases the optional backends compile (connectome.load(dataset="fafb" | "banc")).
  # Public Codex releases, CC BY 4.0; the files flyverse reads and their SHA-256 hashes are in
  # flyverse/data/manifest.json. Cite the original reconstruction papers alongside the release.
  - type: data
    title: "FlyWire FAFB v783: the adult Drosophila melanogaster female brain connectome (Codex public release)"
    authors:
      - name: "FlyWire Consortium"
    year: 2024
    version: "783"
    url: "https://codex.flywire.ai/"
    notes: "Princeton / FlyWire. Used as the complete female optic lobe and brain backend."

  - type: data
    title: "BANC v888: the adult Drosophila melanogaster female brain and ventral nerve cord connectome (Codex public release)"
    authors:
      - name: "FlyWire Consortium"
    year: 2025
    version: "888"
    url: "https://codex.flywire.ai/"
    notes: "Used as the whole-animal female backend, including verified transmitter calls."

  # The neuron model flyverse's LIF follows: v_rest = v_reset = -52 mV, v_th = -45 mV, tau_m = 20 ms,
  # tau_syn = 5 ms, refractory 2.2 ms, delay 1.8 ms, w = 0.275 mV x synapse count x sign (docs/NOTES.md).
  - type: article
    title: "A Drosophila computational brain model reveals sensorimotor processing"
